{%- doc -%}
  Declares a high-priority preload for the hero video's poster frame.

  The homepage's LCP is that poster. `video_tag` emits it as a `poster`
  attribute, which the browser cannot discover until it has parsed the <video>
  element, and on the homepage it then loses the early bandwidth race to three
  app-injected script preloads (jquery, purify, mustache) that this theme does
  not control. PageSpeed reports it as "LCP request discovery". Preloading the
  same image moves that discovery into the head.

  The href MUST resolve to the identical URL `video_tag` builds from the same
  `image_size`, or the browser downloads the poster twice and this is worse than
  doing nothing. That is the `_<size>` suffix form produced by `img_url` -- NOT
  `image_url: width: <n>`, which produces `?width=<n>`. Hence the deprecated
  filter here, deliberately, as in sections/ona-training-banner.liquid. `size`
  is passed in rather than written here so it cannot drift from the `image_size`
  the section's own `video_tag` calls use.

  Scoping follows which element actually paints, not which posters exist. When
  mobile has no media of its own (`fallback_to_desktop`) the desktop element is
  shown at every width, so its preload is left unscoped. Otherwise the desktop
  element is `display: none` below the breakpoint and each preload is narrowed
  to the half that shows it -- exact complements, never both and never neither.
  A distinct mobile *image* needs nothing from here: `image_tag` already carries
  `fetchpriority` on the first section.

  Only the first section is preloaded; below the fold there is nothing to win.
  That test lives here rather than at the call site because
  sections/hero.liquid sits exactly on theme-check's Liquid complexity ceiling
  of 120 -- a single added `if` there tips it over, so every decision this
  feature needs is on this side of the render.

  @param section_index - the section's position on the page; only 1 preloads
  @param size - the `image_size` the section's `video_tag` calls use, e.g. '1600x'
  @param mobile_max - the largest viewport width that paints the mobile element
  @param desktop_media - media type of the desktop slot ('video' when there is a poster)
  @param mobile_media - media type of the mobile slot
  @param fallback_to_desktop - true when mobile has no media of its own
  @param desktop_video - the desktop video object
  @param mobile_video - the mobile video object
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- if section_index == 1 -%}
  {%- if desktop_media == 'video' and desktop_video.preview_image != blank -%}
    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="image"
      href="{{ desktop_video.preview_image | img_url: size }}"
      fetchpriority="high"
      {%- unless fallback_to_desktop %}
        media="not all and (max-width: {{ mobile_max }}px)"
      {%- endunless %}
    >
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- unless fallback_to_desktop -%}
    {%- if mobile_media == 'video' and mobile_video.preview_image != blank -%}
      <link
        rel="preload"
        as="image"
        href="{{ mobile_video.preview_image | img_url: size }}"
        fetchpriority="high"
        media="(max-width: {{ mobile_max }}px)"
      >
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
{%- endif -%}
